call_details.response:
The board state has 17 pieces already placed (9 x and 8 o), with o to move. Columns 2 is completely full and therefore illegal; the legal landing squares are (4,0), (1,1), (0,3), (4,4), (5,5) and (5,6).

No legal placement of o produces four-in-a-row, so there is no immediate win. Likewise, an exhaustive check of every landing square available to x shows that x also possesses no immediate winning reply; the only live three-in-a-row that x already owns (the diagonal (5,1)-(4,2)-(3,3)) is still two plies away from becoming playable. Consequently the present move is a pure developmental choice.

Among the six legal squares the placement (4,4) is uniquely strong:

- it simultaneously lengthens two separate open chains already owned by o (the horizontal pair on row 4 and the vertical pair in column 4);
- each of those chains retains at least two free terminal squares, giving o multiple independent routes to a future four;
- the square lies in the most central available column, maximising the number of winning lines that can still pass through it;
- the opponent’s pending diagonal threat that ends at (2,4) cannot be converted into a tempo gain or a forced win against this move, because the reply that would activate that threat also hands o a useful open three on row 2. Rational play therefore leaves column 4 untouched after the move, preserving o’s newly-created twos.

All other legal landings produce strictly fewer (or more tightly blocked) open chains and sit farther from the centre. Hence column 4 is the unique optimal choice.

Final Answer: 4

